longstanding conjecture of Erdős and Heilbronn. ... If \\(T\\) is a linear operator, we denote by \\(P_T\\) the minimal polynomial of \\(T\\), by \\(\\sigma(T)\\) the spectrum of \\(T\\) (the \\(n\\)-tuple of characteristic roots of \\(T\\) in \\(\\overline{\\mathbb{F}}\\), the algebraic closure of \\(\\mathbb{F}\\)) and by \\(I\\) the identity linear operator.\n*LINEAR ALGEBRAIC TECHNIQUES IN ADDITIVE THEORY* — 107\n\nThe theorem of Cauchy-Davenport can be easily generalized to addition of \\(n\\) sets of residues, giving\n\n\\[\n|A_1 + A_2 + \\cdots + A_m| \\ge \\min\\{p,\\ |A_1| + \\cdots + |A_m| - m + 1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich in our notation is equivalent to\n\n\\[\n|s_{1,m}(A_1,\\ldots,A_m)| \\ge \\min\\{p,\\ |A_1| + \\cdots + |A_m| - m + 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe would like to find similar bounds for all other powers \\(k\\).\n\n### 3. Linear Algebraic Techniques\n\nDias da Silva and Hamidoune[8] were the first to apply linear algebraic techniques to number theory, proving a longstanding conjecture of Erdős and Heilbronn. And the starting point was to present a new proof[7] for the Cauchy-Davenport Theorem, as a consequence of a result on lower bounds for the degree of the minimal polynomial of the linear operator (a *Kronecker sum*)\n\n\\[\nf \\otimes I_W + I_V \\otimes g \\in L(V \\otimes W, V \\otimes W),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(V\\) and \\(W\\) are vector spaces of finite dimension over a field.\n\nLet us start with all the necessary definitions and notations. Let \\(V_i\\) be a vector space of finite dimension over \\(\\mathbb{F}\\) (an arbitrary field of characteristic \\(p\\), a prime number, if it is of finite characteristic, or \\(p=\\infty\\) otherwise). Let \\(L(V_i,V_i)\\) be the \\(\\mathbb{F}\\)-algebra of linear operators on \\(V_i\\). We denote by \\(V_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes V_m\\) the tensor product of \\(V_1,\\ldots,V_m\\). If \\(T\\) is a linear operator, we denote by \\(P_T\\) the minimal polynomial of \\(T\\), by \\(\\sigma(T)\\) the spectrum of \\(T\\) (the \\(n\\)-tuple of characteristic roots of \\(T\\) in \\(\\overline{\\mathbb{F}}\\), the algebraic closure of \\(\\mathbb{F}\\)) and by \\(I\\) the identity linear operator.\n\nLet \\(A_1,\\ldots,A_m\\) be algebras, and define, for \\(\\omega\\in Q_{k,m}\\), the map \\(\\delta_\\omega\\) from \\(A_1\\times\\cdots\\times A_m\\) to \\(A_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes A_m\\), as\n\n\\[\n\\delta_\\omega(a_1,\\ldots,a_m)=u_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes u_m,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(u_j=a_j\\) if \\(j\\in\\operatorname{Im}(\\omega)\\), or \\(u_j=1_{A_j}\\) otherwise.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorial Nullstellensatz (https://studyres.com/doc/15227992/combinatorial-nullstellensatz)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;     The Cauchy-Davenport Theorem, which has numerous applications in Additive Number Theory, ...     the theorem as a discrete analogue of a conjecture of Khintchine (which was proved a few years ...     n = 2, S1 = A, S2 = B), there is an a ∈ A and a b ∈ B so that f (a, b) 6= 0, contradicting (5) and\n    The Cauchy-Davenport Theorem, which has numerous applications in Additive Number Theory,\n    is the following.\n    Theorem 3.2 ([20]) If p is a prime, and A, B are two nonempty subsets of Zp , then\n    |A + B| ≥ min{p, |A| + |B| − 1}.\n    4\n    Cauchy proved this theorem in 1813, and applied it to give a new proof to a lemma of Lagrange in his\n    well known 1770 paper that shows that any integer is a sum of four squares. Davenport formulated\n    the theorem as a discrete analogue of a conjecture of Khintchine (which was proved a few years\n    later by H. Mann) about the Schnirelman density of the sum of two sequences of integers. There\n    are numerous extensions of this result, see, e.g., [45]. The proofs of Theorem 3.2 given by Cauchy\n    and Davenport are based on the same combinatorial idea, and apply induction on |B|. A different,\n    algebraic proof has recently been found by the authors of [10], [11], and its main advantage is that\n    it extends easily and gives several related results. As shown below, this proof can be described as a\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectures | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/conjectures)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Conjecture 1.13 as stated is false: at $p=1$, $n=2$ and $(z_1,z_2)=(1,2)$ give equality although $z_1\\ne z_2$.\n\nFurther frontiers Counterexample found First preprint 2025 Tang–Zhang: real-order reciprocal critical-point moments The reciprocal critical-point moment has the sharp Tang–Zhang coefficient for every real p at least one.Refuted\n\nThe question Zhang, Sharp Schoenberg type inequalities and the de Bruin–Sharma problem, arXiv:2508.10341v3, §1.3, Conjecture 1.13.\n\nWhy it matters Conjecture 1.13 asks for a sharp real-order extension of reciprocal critical-point inequalities.\n\nThe idea The polynomial and its multiplicity-preserving reciprocal-root spectral reduction are reused from TangZhangOrderMinusTwo.\n\nOur contribution The reciprocal critical-point moment has the sharp Tang–Zhang coefficient for every real p at least one.\n\nVerification details\n\nRelease source record Refuted in Lean\n\nOpen problem\n\n### Tang–Zhang: real-order reciprocal critical-point moments\n\nQ. Tang and T. Zhang, Sharp Schoenberg type inequalities and the de Bruin–Sharma problem, arXiv:2508.10341v3, §1.3, Conjecture 1.13. The source is tang2025schoenberg.\n\n> Conjecture 1.13. Let P(z) = z ∏_{j=1}^n (z − z_j), z_j ∈ C \\ {0}, and let w_1, …, w_n be the zeros of P′(z). Then for every real number p ≥ 1 we have Σ_{k=1}^n 1/|w_k|^p ≤ ((n+1)^p + n − 1)/n · Σ_{j=1}^n 1/|z_j|^p. Moreover, equality holds if and only if z_1 = ⋯ = z_n.\n\nFor every natural $n\\ge1$ and every family $z_1,\\ldots,z_n\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus{0}$, put\n\n$$ P(Z)=Z\\prod_{j=1}^n(Z-z_j),\\qquad c(n,p)=\\frac{(n+1)^p+n-1}{n}. $$\n\nLet $w_1,\\ldots,w_n$ be all roots of $P'$, counted with algebraic multiplicity. No distinctness, simplicity, reality or common argument hypothesis is imposed on the nonzero roots. The result proves the following three clauses:\n\n  1. For every real $p\\ge1$, $$\\sum_{k=1}^n|w_k|^{-p}\\le c(n,p)\\sum_{j=1}^n|z_j|^{-p}.$$\n  2. For every real $p>1$, equality holds if and only if $z_i=z_j$ for every $i,j$.\n  3. Conjecture 1.13 as stated is false: at $p=1$, $n=2$ and $(z_1,z_2)=(1,2)$ give equality although $z_1\\ne z_2$.\n\nThe formal `claim` is the conjunction of (i), (ii), and `¬ conjecture`, where the original conjecture has its full dimension, root and order quantifiers:\n    \n    `def conjecture : Prop :=\n        ∀ (n : ℕ) (hn : 1 ≤ n) (z : Fin n → ℂ), (∀ j, z j ≠ 0) → ∀ p : ℝ, 1 ≤ p →\n          (((Polynomial.derivative (P z)).roots.map (fun w => ‖w‖ ^ (-p))).sum ≤\n            c n p * ∑ j, ‖z j‖ ^ (-p)) ∧\n          ((((Polynomial.derivative (P z)).roots.map (fun w => ‖w‖ ^ (-p))).sum =\n            c n p * ∑ j, ‖z j‖ ^ (-p)) ↔ ∀ i j, z i = z j)\n    `\n\nEvery norm power is a real `Real.rpow`, including nonintegral orders. The named conjecture is refuted by its endpoint equality clause. The sharp inequality for all $p≥1$ and the strict-order equality case are proved in the same result.\n\nProof explanation Original questionRelease problem dossier\n\nProof record\n\nModule present in current Truth release; resolution evidence is pinned below.\n\nD5/S3/Zeros/CriticalPoints/TangZhangRealOrder.result\n\nRead in mdBook\n\nFrozen module record ``\n\nEvidence snapshot: 63d6fd5f7de3e06815a0e6a7e0cbd9f23c638e64. The linked mdBook follows upstream development.\n\n  * Historical context\n\nFirst preprint year: 2025.\n\nRead source ↗\nWhy this order\n\n  * Problem significance · 2/4 · 30% weight\n\nAn explicitly identified research question in the original source.\n\nSource 1 · Source 2\n  * Conceptual contribution · 2/4 · 20% weight\n\nThe delivered argument gives a structural char